>
  <h2>The situation</h2>
  <p>A growing AI platform with a small team, an offshore design group, and a roadmap that had drifted away from what customers were asking for. Every new screen was being designed and built from scratch.</p>

  <h2>What I did</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Built and led a tokenized design system in Figma and React, so designers and engineers worked from the same parts and could prototype in real time.</li>
    <li>Directed the offshore design team and taught them design systems practice.</li>
    <li>Ran generative interviews with HR and DEI leaders and turned them into a service blueprint that brought the roadmap back in line with customer needs. Used Kano analysis to decide what to build first.</li>
    <li>Designed the data visualizations and the client presentations for the product.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What changed</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Development cycles about 30% faster.</li>
    <li>The product evolved from a hiring tool into a full people-analytics platform.</li>
    <li>It won a 2023 SXSW Innovation Award, and Included was later acquired by Phenom.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why it matters for your business</h2>
  <blockquote>A system of reusable parts beats heroic one-offs.</blockquote>
  <p>That's true for design systems, and it's just as true for AI. The prompt libraries, context files and skills I build for clients follow the same idea: write the standard once, reuse it everywhere, and improve it in one place.</p>
</CaseStudy>
